{
  "concept": "A change of variables T(u,v) = (x(u,v), y(u,v)) sends a small rectangle S in the uv-plane to a curved region R in the xy-plane, and the Jacobian determinant is the local factor by which that map stretches area.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Left panel: uv-coordinate axes with arrowheads, u-axis labeled 'u' (horizontal), v-axis labeled 'v' (vertical)",
    "Left panel: two vertical blue grid lines labeled 'u = u₀' and 'u = u₀ + Δu'",
    "Left panel: two horizontal blue grid lines labeled 'v = v₀' and 'v = v₀ + Δv'",
    "Left panel: light-blue filled rectangle S bounded by those four lines, labeled 'S' at its center",
    "Left panel: four blue corner dots at the intersections of the grid lines",
    "Left panel: corner label '(u₀, v₀)' at the lower-left dot, side labels 'Δu' (bottom edge) and 'Δv' (right edge)",
    "Center: large curved cream/tan arrow with grey outline pointing left-to-right, labeled 'T' (the transformation)",
    "Right panel: xy-coordinate axes with arrowheads, 'x' (horizontal) and 'y' (vertical)",
    "Right panel: four blue curved boundary arcs forming a curvilinear quadrilateral, each extending slightly past the corners",
    "Right panel: light-blue filled curved region R, labeled 'R' at its center",
    "Right panel: curve labels 'Image of u = u₀', 'Image of u = u₀ + Δu', 'Image of v = v₀', 'Image of v = v₀ + Δv'",
    "Optional overlay: tangent vectors (∂r/∂u)Δu and (∂r/∂v)Δv at the image of (u₀,v₀) with the dashed approximating parallelogram",
    "Optional readout box: area(S) = ΔuΔv, area(R) ≈ |J(u₀,v₀)| ΔuΔv, and the measured ratio area(R)/area(S)"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "transformation",
      "type": "equation_input",
      "teaches": "The shape of the image region R is entirely dictated by the transformation formulas; different formulas bend and stretch the same rectangle in completely different ways.",
      "label": "Transformation T: (u,v) → (x,y)",
      "fields": [
        {
          "id": "xExpr",
          "label": "x(u,v) =",
          "default": "u*cos(v)",
          "variables": ["u", "v"],
          "domain": [0, 3],
          "role": "x-component of the map T"
        },
        {
          "id": "yExpr",
          "label": "y(u,v) =",
          "default": "u*sin(v)",
          "variables": ["u", "v"],
          "domain": [0, 3],
          "role": "y-component of the map T"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "dragCorners",
      "type": "drag",
      "teaches": "Moving the rectangle S to a different place in the uv-plane changes how strongly its image is stretched — the distortion factor depends on WHERE you are, not just on the formula.",
      "teachesDetail": "Drag the lower-left corner (u₀,v₀) to translate S, or drag the upper-right corner to resize Δu and Δv; R redraws instantly."
    },
    {
      "id": "cellSize",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "As Δu and Δv shrink, the curved image R becomes indistinguishable from a straight parallelogram and the area ratio settles down to the single number |J(u₀,v₀)|.",
      "label": "Cell size (Δu = Δv)",
      "range": [0.05, 1.2, 0.05],
      "default": 0.8
    },
    {
      "id": "showJacobian",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "The tangent vectors (∂r/∂u)Δu and (∂r/∂v)Δv span a parallelogram whose area |J|ΔuΔv approximates area(R) — this is exactly where the Jacobian factor in the change-of-variables formula comes from.",
      "label": "Overlay",
      "options": ["Image region only", "Tangent parallelogram + area readout"],
      "default": "Image region only"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "A rectangle in the uv-plane",
      "narration": "On the left, the four lines u = u₀, u = u₀+Δu, v = v₀, v = v₀+Δv cut out a plain rectangle S of area ΔuΔv. Nothing curved yet.",
      "state": { "cellSize": 0.8, "showJacobian": "Image region only" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Apply the transformation",
      "narration": "The polar map x = u·cos v, y = u·sin v carries each edge of S to a curve: the u = constant edges become circular arcs, the v = constant edges become radial segments. The image R is a curved quadrilateral, clearly not the same area as S.",
      "state": { "cellSize": 0.8, "showJacobian": "Image region only" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Move the cell around",
      "narration": "Drag the corner (u₀,v₀) outward. The same rectangle produces a much larger image far from the origin — the stretching factor is a function of position.",
      "state": { "cellSize": 0.8, "showJacobian": "Image region only" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Shrink the cell",
      "narration": "Reduce Δu and Δv. The curvature of R's edges becomes invisible and R looks like a straight-sided parallelogram.",
      "state": { "cellSize": 0.15, "showJacobian": "Image region only" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Read off the Jacobian",
      "narration": "Turn on the overlay: the tangent vectors (∂r/∂u)Δu and (∂r/∂v)Δv span a parallelogram of area |∂(x,y)/∂(u,v)|·ΔuΔv, and the measured ratio area(R)/area(S) matches |J| to within a hair.",
      "state": { "cellSize": 0.15, "showJacobian": "Tangent parallelogram + area readout" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Change the formula",
      "narration": "Edit x(u,v) and y(u,v) — try x = u² − v², y = 2uv. The rectangle S is untouched, but R takes a new shape and a new area ratio: the Jacobian is just bookkeeping for whatever your formula does to area.",
      "state": { "cellSize": 0.3, "showJacobian": "Tangent parallelogram + area readout" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Under a smooth change of variables, an infinitesimal rectangle of area ΔuΔv maps to a nearly-parallelogram of area |∂(x,y)/∂(u,v)|·ΔuΔv — the Jacobian is nothing more than the local area-stretching factor, which is why dA = |J| du dv.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two side-by-side panels on a white background, separated by a wide curved cream/tan arrow with a grey outline sweeping left to right (label it 'T'). Both panels show only the first-quadrant portion of black axes with arrowheads, axis letters set in italic serif (u, v on the left; x, y on the right). Left panel: four medium-blue lines (uniform stroke) extending past the rectangle they bound, enclosing a pale-blue translucent rectangle labeled italic 'S' at its center; solid blue dots at the four intersections; text labels 'u = u₀' and 'u = u₀ + Δu' above the two vertical lines, 'v = v₀' and 'v = v₀ + Δv' to the right of the two horizontal lines, '(u₀, v₀)' below the lower-left corner, 'Δu' under the bottom edge and 'Δv' beside the right edge. Right panel: the same pale-blue fill bounded by four smooth blue arcs that bow outward/inward and overshoot each corner a little, labeled italic 'R' at the center; four text labels placed outside the region near the free ends of each arc: 'Image of u = u₀' (upper left), 'Image of u = u₀ + Δu' (upper right), 'Image of v = v₀ + Δv' (middle right), 'Image of v = v₀' (lower right). Use consistent hues: boundary curves and grid lines in a saturated cyan-blue, fills in a light desaturated blue, text in black serif with proper subscripts and Greek deltas. When the tangent-parallelogram overlay is active, draw two arrows in a contrasting color (e.g. orange/red) from the image of (u₀,v₀) and a dashed outline of the parallelogram they span, plus a small readout panel giving area(S), area(R), the ratio, and |J| at (u₀,v₀)."
}

